WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A toy designer has made a small treasure chest, but the
cardboard wrapper will not close.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.320 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One panel is too short and another flap covers the opening
twice.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.880 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Could you become the packaging engineer who fixes it?

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.814 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our clues will be face shapes, matching edges and careful
testing.

00:00:16.814 --> 00:00:17.294 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to design and test nets with matching
face dimensions.

00:00:17.294 --> 00:00:27.633 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will build an explanation using models, rather than
memorising a rule without understanding it.

00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:32.273 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to design and test nets with matching
face dimensions.

00:00:32.273 --> 00:00:37.875 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As you investigate, connect what you can see, what you can
make, and the mathematical language you use.

00:00:37.875 --> 00:00:40.475 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
First, I can match joining edge lengths.

00:00:40.475 --> 00:00:43.555 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Second, I can build a rectangular-prism net.

00:00:43.555 --> 00:00:46.315 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Third, I can test and improve a design.

00:00:46.315 --> 00:00:49.814 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep these goals in mind as you explain each discovery.

00:00:49.814 --> 00:00:54.574 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Recall that a net is a connected flat arrangement that folds
into an object.

00:00:54.574 --> 00:00:57.734 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today the faces do not have to be equal squares.

00:00:57.734 --> 00:01:02.534 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine opening a rectangular carton along carefully chosen
edges.

00:01:02.534 --> 00:01:08.759 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Opposite faces match, while neighbouring faces may have
different dimensions that share just one edge length.

00:01:08.759 --> 00:01:15.559 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will design a rectangular prism that is four centimetres
long, three centimetres wide and two centimetres high.

00:01:15.559 --> 00:01:18.919 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
These measurements describe three different directions.

00:01:18.919 --> 00:01:22.839 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before drawing, point to each direction on an imagined
carton.

00:01:22.839 --> 00:01:27.174 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Swapping a height label with a length label would change
which panels must meet.

00:01:27.174 --> 00:01:30.494 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The top and bottom are four by three rectangles.

00:01:30.494 --> 00:01:33.654 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The front and back are four by two rectangles.

00:01:33.654 --> 00:01:36.614 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The two ends are three by two rectangles.

00:01:36.614 --> 00:01:40.294 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This gives three matching pairs and six faces altogether.

00:01:40.294 --> 00:01:45.812 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Notice that each direction appears on more than one face
because faces share edges.

00:01:45.812 --> 00:01:48.412 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Lay out four side panels in one row.

00:01:48.412 --> 00:01:51.652 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Their widths are four, three, four and three centimetres.

00:01:51.652 --> 00:01:56.172 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each panel is two centimetres high, so their shared joining
edges match.

00:01:56.172 --> 00:02:01.871 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
When the row wraps around, the final free edge meets the
first free edge to complete the sides.

00:02:01.871 --> 00:02:06.711 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The side strip is ready, but the carton still has an opening
at the top and bottom.

00:02:06.711 --> 00:02:10.031 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which pair of rectangles should close those openings?

00:02:10.031 --> 00:02:15.951 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Think about the carton’s length and width, rather than
choosing a panel simply because it is available.

00:02:15.951 --> 00:02:18.905 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict the dimensions before we continue.

00:02:23.905 --> 00:02:26.505 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We need two four by three rectangles.

00:02:26.505 --> 00:02:30.625 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Attach one above and one below the first four by two side
panel.

00:02:30.625 --> 00:02:34.665 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each attached join is four centimetres long on both faces.

00:02:34.665 --> 00:02:40.682 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The depth of the closing panel is three centimetres,
matching the distance across the carton’s opening.

00:02:40.682 --> 00:02:44.602 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our drawing now has four side panels and two closing panels.

00:02:44.602 --> 00:02:44.962 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Trace the boundary and imagine each panel folding to its
destination.

00:02:44.962 --> 00:02:52.402 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The two four by three panels become opposite faces.

00:02:52.402 --> 00:02:57.949 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
They must not both try to cover the same opening, and the
final side-panel seam must still meet.

00:02:57.949 --> 00:03:01.909 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use one grid square for one centimetre when you draw this
design.

00:03:01.909 --> 00:03:06.709 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A four-centimetre edge then spans four grid spaces wherever
it appears.

00:03:06.709 --> 00:03:10.269 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keeping one scale lets the picture support the reasoning.

00:03:10.269 --> 00:03:17.252 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Drawing every rectangle as an identical square would hide
the important dimensional differences in this example.

00:03:17.252 --> 00:03:21.812 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Suppose the designer uses a four by two rectangle as the
top.

00:03:21.812 --> 00:03:23.492 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
There are still six panels, but the top needs a depth of
three centimetres.

00:03:23.492 --> 00:03:29.412 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The shorter panel leaves part of the opening uncovered.

00:03:29.412 --> 00:03:34.288 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Adding a label saying top does not change the actual size of
the cardboard.

00:03:34.288 --> 00:03:37.568 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Replace that top with a four by three rectangle.

00:03:37.568 --> 00:03:42.448 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep the correct side strip and the correct bottom: we do
not need to redesign everything.

00:03:42.448 --> 00:03:45.129 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This is a useful engineering habit.

00:03:45.129 --> 00:03:51.543 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Identify the precise mismatch, make the smallest justified
correction, and test the altered design again.

00:03:51.543 --> 00:03:54.463 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For a second design, start with a square base.

00:03:54.463 --> 00:03:57.543 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Attach one matching isosceles triangle to each side of the
square.

00:03:57.543 --> 00:03:58.183 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each triangle base must match its square edge.

00:03:58.183 --> 00:04:10.278 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The four triangle tips rise towards one apex when folded,
producing a square-based pyramid rather than a prism with
two matching ends.

00:04:10.278 --> 00:04:15.118 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The rising sides of neighbouring triangles must meet when
the pyramid closes.

00:04:15.118 --> 00:04:19.998 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Using four matching triangles with suitable height gives a
consistent design.

00:04:19.998 --> 00:04:25.158 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Random triangles with the correct base length may still have
incompatible side lengths.

00:04:25.158 --> 00:04:29.553 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We need to consider the edges that become joins after
folding as well.

00:04:29.553 --> 00:04:34.433 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before counting along, predict how many faces belong in this
pyramid net.

00:04:34.433 --> 00:04:37.993 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Separate the square base from the triangular side faces.

00:04:37.993 --> 00:04:43.553 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Does the apex need a separate tiny face, or is it a point
where existing faces meet?

00:04:43.553 --> 00:04:48.000 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Explain your prediction using the object rather than the
drawing alone.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:58.200 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
There is one square base and four triangular faces, making
five faces altogether.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:01.320 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The apex is a vertex, not an extra face.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:03.960 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The triangles meet there when folded.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:11.550 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This connects our earlier vocabulary about faces and
vertices to a practical decision about how many cardboard
panels to cut.

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:15.790 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A prism has two matching parallel ends joined by side faces.

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:20.190 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A pyramid has a base and triangular faces meeting at an
apex.

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:20.510 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose a package by considering the object it must contain,
then design the appropriate faces.

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:31.188 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A triangular outline somewhere in a drawing does not
identify the whole solid.

00:05:31.188 --> 00:05:38.108 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Write down the intended object, the number and shape of its
faces, and the dimensions of important joining edges.

00:05:38.108 --> 00:05:39.988 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Sketch the net before cutting.

00:05:39.988 --> 00:05:43.987 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
After folding, record any gaps or overlaps and where they
occur.

00:05:43.987 --> 00:05:49.169 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This turns an unsuccessful trial into useful information for
the next version.

00:05:49.169 --> 00:05:54.489 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Design a different connected net for this same four by three
by two centimetre carton.

00:05:54.489 --> 00:06:00.209 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You may change which edges remain joined, but keep every
panel at its required dimensions.

00:06:00.209 --> 00:06:07.442 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict whether your arrangement will close without overlap,
and check all joining edges before making a paper prototype.

00:06:07.442 --> 00:06:13.842 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For this carton, the opposite face pairs remain four by
three, four by two, and three by two centimetres.

00:06:13.842 --> 00:06:18.322 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Rearranging a net changes its connections, not the required
faces.

00:06:18.322 --> 00:06:26.196 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare your new arrangement with the reference model and
explain why its folds should reach the correct positions
without gaps or collisions.

00:06:26.196 --> 00:06:32.036 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can now plan face pairs, match joining edges and test a
net against its intended object.

00:06:32.036 --> 00:06:37.796 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Return to the treasure-chest wrapper: what evidence would
convince its designer that your correction works?

00:06:37.796 --> 00:06:42.742 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use a labelled net and a folding explanation, then try a new
package of your own.
